# Call, Cast, and Await

## Overview

You have a running Agent and a Signal to send. Jido gives you three ways to
send it, and each one confirms something different. This Livebook sends
Signals with `call/3`, `cast/3`, and `submit/3` plus `await/2`, and shows what
each result means when the work is slow or fails.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, agent, directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, signal)   # waits for commit
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(server, signal)                        # sent, nothing more
pending = Jido.AgentServer.submit(server, signal)                  # send now
{:ok, agent, directives} = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending)         # collect later
```

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## A Worker Agent

The `job.run` route sleeps for `ms` milliseconds, then appends the job name
to `done`. The sleep stands in for slow work.

```elixir
defmodule Guides.Worker do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "worker"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{done: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/call-cast-await")

    route "job.run", as: :run do
      action %{name: name, ms: ms},
        name: "run_job",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string(), ms: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}), context: context do
        Process.sleep(ms)
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | done: context.agent_state.done ++ [name]}}
      end
    end
  end
end

defmodule Guides.Wait do
  def until(fun, timeout \\ 3_000) do
    deadline = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + timeout
    loop(fun, deadline)
  end

  defp loop(fun, deadline) do
    cond do
      fun.() -> :ok
      System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) > deadline -> :timeout
      true -> Process.sleep(10) && loop(fun, deadline)
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _} = Jido.start()
{:ok, worker} = Jido.start_agent(Guides.Worker, id: "worker-1")
```

`Guides.Wait.until/2` polls a condition every 10 ms for up to 3 seconds.
The last line returns `{:ok, pid}`.

## What Each Confirms

| Function | Returns | Confirms |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `call/3` | `{:ok, agent, directives}` or `{:error, reason}` | The Turn committed, or failed before commit. |
| `cast/3` | `:ok` or `{:error, reason}` | The message was sent. Not admission, commit, or delivery. |
| `submit/3` | A pending handle or `{:error, reason}` | The request was sent. Not that it started. |
| `await/2` | The same result as `call/3` | Same as `call/3`. |

None of them confirms that Directives finished. A successful reply means the
Server committed. Directives run after the reply.

## Call

`call/3` runs one Turn and waits for the result. The third argument is a
timeout in milliseconds or a keyword list.

```elixir
job = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "a"})
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(worker, job, context: %{request_id: "req-1"})

agent.state.done
```

The result is `["a"]`. The Action received `request_id: "req-1"` in its
context.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `timeout:` | `5_000` | How long you wait for the reply. |
| `admission_timeout:` | `5_000` | How long the Turn can wait in the queue before it starts. `:infinity` disables it. |
| `context:` | `%{}` | Caller data for the Action context. A map, keyword list, or `nil`. |

Context isn't stored in Agent state or in the Signal. The Agent command keys
are reserved:

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.call(worker, job, context: %{agent_state: %{}})
```

The result is `{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}}` with the message
`"Agent command context contains reserved keys"` and
`details: %{keys: [:agent_state]}`. `:turn_id` and `:trace_context` fail with
`":turn_id and :trace_context are reserved"`. An unknown option fails with
`"invalid turn options"`.

A Turn that fails before commit returns its error, and state doesn't change:

```elixir
unknown = Jido.Signal.new!("job.missing", %{}, source: "/guides/call-cast-await")
{:error, error} = Jido.AgentServer.call(worker, unknown)

{error.code, Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done}
```

The result is `{:unmatched_input, ["a"]}`.

## A Timeout Isn't a Cancel

`timeout:` limits how long you wait. It doesn't stop the Turn. When it
expires, the caller exits, as with any OTP call:

```elixir
slow = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "slow", ms: 300})

result =
  try do
    Jido.AgentServer.call(worker, slow, timeout: 50)
  catch
    :exit, {reason, _call} -> {:exit, reason}
  end

:ok = Guides.Wait.until(fn -> "slow" in Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done end)
{result, Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done}
```

The result is `{{:exit, :timeout}, ["a", "slow"]}`. The caller gave up after
50 ms. The Turn committed anyway. To stop pre-commit work, use
`Jido.AgentServer.cancel/2`; see
[Admission and Deadlines](agent-server-admission.md).

## Admission Deadlines

The Server runs one Turn at a time. `admission_timeout:` limits how long your
Turn waits behind others before it starts. Queue a slow job with a cast, then
call with a 50 ms admission deadline:

```elixir
slow_2 = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "slow-2", ms: 300})
behind = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "behind"})

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(worker, slow_2)
admission = Jido.AgentServer.call(worker, behind, admission_timeout: 50, timeout: 2_000)

:ok = Guides.Wait.until(fn -> "slow-2" in Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done end)
{admission, Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done}
```

The result is `{{:error, :admission_timeout}, ["a", "slow", "slow-2"]}`. The
`behind` job never ran. A finite admission deadline requires a caller on the
Server's node.

## Cast

`cast/3` sends the Signal and returns. It accepts only `context:`. It has no
admission deadline.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(worker, unknown)
:ok = Guides.Wait.until(fn -> Jido.AgentServer.status(worker).last_outcome.signal_id == unknown.id end)

Map.take(Jido.AgentServer.status(worker).last_outcome, [:status, :stage, :committed?])
```

The result is `%{status: :failed, stage: :preparing, committed?: false}`. The
cast returned `:ok` even though the Turn failed. The Server logs
`AgentServer cast turn failed` with the Turn ID. Read `status/2` when you
need the outcome of a cast.

`cast/3` validates before sending. A value that isn't a Signal returns
`{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{message: "Agent command must contain a Jido.Signal"}}`.
When the queue reaches `max_postponed_signals:` (default 1,000), the Server
drops further casts and logs each one.

## Submit and Await

`submit/3` sends the Turn and returns a handle at once. `await/2` collects the
result later, from the same process. `submit/3` accepts `context:` and
`admission_timeout:`.

```elixir
job = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "submitted", ms: 200})
pending = Jido.AgentServer.submit(worker, job)

first = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 10)
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 2_000)

{first, List.last(agent.state.done)}
```

The result is `{{:error, :timeout}, "submitted"}`. An `await/2` timeout
returns `{:error, :timeout}` without losing the reply. Call it again to keep
waiting. The default wait is 5,000 ms.

The handle belongs to the submitting process:

```elixir
Task.async(fn -> Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 10) end) |> Task.await()
```

The result is `{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}}` with the message
`"await must run in the process that submitted the turn"`.

Rules for a handle:

- You collect a result once. Don't await a handle after you collect it.
- The handle isn't durable and can't move to another process.
- `abandon/1` discards the reply and returns `:ok`. It doesn't cancel the
  Turn.
- If the Server stops before replying, `await/2` returns
  `{:error, %Jido.Error.ExecutionError{}}` with
  `"Agent server stopped before the turn replied"`.

```elixir
job = Guides.Worker.run_signal!(%{name: "abandoned"})
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.abandon(Jido.AgentServer.submit(worker, job))
:ok = Guides.Wait.until(fn -> "abandoned" in Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done end)

List.last(Jido.AgentServer.agent(worker).state.done)
```

The result is `"abandoned"`. The Turn ran after you abandoned its reply.

## Send Through a Ref

An instance module built with `use Jido` and a `namespace:` has Ref forms:
`call/3`, `cast/2`, `submit/3`, and `await/2`. Each call resolves the Ref to
the current process. The Ref form of `cast/2` takes no options.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, ref} = MyApp.Jido.agent_ref("worker-1")
{:ok, agent, []} = MyApp.Jido.call(ref, job, timeout: 1_000)
pending = MyApp.Jido.submit(ref, job)
{:ok, agent, []} = MyApp.Jido.await(pending)
```

When the Ref doesn't resolve, these return `{:error, :not_found}` or
`{:error, :recovering}`. See
[Start and Address Agents](start-and-address-live-agents.livemd).

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(worker)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

